#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:5
#: 21ad78cdb6934cecac90a88d11156123
msgid "Tips For New Pilots"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:7
#: 0f63529f709a42668d2c74edd2c9e2ff
msgid "This page gives some advice on initial flying strategies for those with limited or no previous experience flying RC aircraft or multicopters."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:11
#: 3205bd937f644e39a1c893240a998396
msgid "First flight"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:13
#: cf43ee598a6c4ce6a464d51c8694010e
msgid "Place the copter on level ground and connect the battery — do not move the copter until gyroscope calibration is completed (LED's flashing red and blue). Ensure that your RC mode toggle switch is in Stabilize mode. Slowly raise the throttle until the copter just lifts off the ground."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:18
#: edc98793fb6143a2b1dcdaa5de090301
msgid "If it seems like it's going to flip or otherwise isn't lifting straight up it is possible that you've set the wrong +/x orientation, hooked up your motors in the wrong order or have the prop direction or pusher/puller order wrong. You can test this by following the instructions in :ref:`connect-escs-and-motors_testing_motor_spin_directions`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:24
#: d82e52b651f14d3d8993ca2725e72da1
msgid "If any of the controls are reversed (pitch or roll are backwards, meaning the copter moves in the opposite direction as your stick), you'll need to reverse the affected channel at your RC transmitter side. Please see your RC transmitter's manual if you're not familiar with the process. It's a good idea to re-do your RC calibration in the Mission Planner after you've reversed a channel."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:31
#: ae56b2b5a0a34afa844aa45010145755
msgid "If it lifts off smoothly, you may see a little yaw. That shouldn't be more than about 30 degrees and will correct itself with more flying time. There should be no pronounced wobble (if there is, you may have an unbalanced prop or out-of-true motor)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:36
#: d919589561f544b58777cd0d509d595f
msgid "The copter should also tend to stay in place, and you shouldn't have to fight strong tendencies to move left or right or forward or back. If you do see that behavior, do NOT use your RC trims to correct (this just throws off the calibration). Instead, it probably means that the copter may not have been completely flat during calibration or the airframe is out of true (one motor tilted). Land and correct that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:43
#: 5595cc8565bd4e40b59fbe6bbe2667b2
msgid "If you're having trouble and the copter is not hovering smoothly, run through the :ref:`troubleshooting guide <troubleshooting>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:46
#: cb7a880b758c48a89f4820e7c7f4c193
msgid "Assuming that all is fine so far, you're ready for more advanced modes, such as Alt Hold and Loiter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:50
#: 675477e8f98a4161ba8a60e57ccb2092
msgid "Tips for your first flights"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:52
#: 596adbdaa67a47abb3d889b60d771c30
msgid "Make sure you are in a wind free environment (wind will play against you on in air auto trim)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:54
#: 9787e3f10c3f48239bb1ab8199aaeb43
msgid "Make sure you have no trim on your Radio (the autopilot is what we want to trim, the radio should never get trimmed)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:56
#: 435fd74286004661921c661c7de217c4
msgid "Hold the copter still and level after connecting the battery to allow the gyroscopes to initialize."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:58
#: 8d90471f70a84d6db736e41184638d0f
msgid "Get above ground effect, around 3-4 feet is enough on most models."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:59
#: 69e2598390994d7a84d6efc414ec7aa2
msgid "Make sure to practice a lot before you actually try the auto trim to find the sweet spot on the Radio right stick to have the copter super stable and not moving (This is where wind affect your inputs)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:63
#: fbe18544089340599feb8ac31ce6266d
msgid "We recommend not starting in Simple mode. Begin your flying in the basic Stabilize mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:65
#: 15b0999281004bfb8b703a09e92fdd5d
msgid "Copter establishes its home position at the time of arming, so arm your copter in the location you wish to designate as the home position."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:70
#: 34a6ee89ebcd4063a69a7cf710f7b564
msgid "Warning about low batteries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:72
#: 1f920a27808f412cac940ba808fbc442
msgid "When flying a VTOL vehicle, you need to keep in mind that it flies much differently than a winged airplane. In an airplane, lift is generated by the wing and you still have control surfaces for maneuvering if the motor were to stop. QuadPlanes and Multicopters generate lift solely from the thrust of the motors, so if your battery is excessively depleted, you have no lift and no control over your vehicle, and it may flip over and crash without a gradual warning. During first flights, fly only short durations until you know your battery consumption and use one of the following batteries monitoring methods to ensure that you always have enough power remaining to safely land your copter (and not ruin the battery)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:83
#: faba23a905004e959f1bd8681bd18a64
msgid "ArduPilot supports Battery voltage monitoring. For more information, visit the :ref:`3DR Power Module for Measuring Voltage and Current <common-3dr-power-module>` page."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:84
#: c4114c50cf7e423c87fa906d72891532
msgid "You may also program your ESCs to use a slow or no cutoff at low voltages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:86
#: 935420eb6eea4cff97cf7b6263fbda16
msgid "For autopilots without power module it is HIGHLY recommended you install a :ref:`Battery Voltage Monitor <common-powermodule-landingpage>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:89
#: 23b242760a7c413b8e81df21f54781ec
msgid "For 3S LiPo batteries, the two wires can be soldered to the P-PCB directly, or you can connect to the balancing plug of your battery."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:92
#: ab38054163cc41479ccf89eeb8533c23
msgid "The battery voltage monitor linked above has a buzzer and LEDs that function as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:129
#: 22f88a3a7cf34b0bacc478b932eb837c
msgid "If you are using a battery monitor, always connect the main LiPo battery wire before connecting the balancing port wire to the APM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:134
#: 5be1b4bd38f8495b9c3a7a3bd89cafb1
msgid "Motors RPM Check"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/ac_tipsfornewpilots.rst:135
#: 87f6f6f628f44a22b6ded4ef49c3907b
msgid "In modes where the vehicle takes off controlling the throttle itself (like AUTO, LOITER, QLOITER, ALTHOLD, etc.), , and ESC telemetry is employed, the :ref:`TKOFF_RPM_MIN <TKOFF_RPM_MIN>` and :ref:`TKOFF_RPM_MAX <TKOFF_RPM_MAX>` are monitored during the motor ramp up. See :ref:`common-tkoff-rpm-min`."
msgstr ""
